{"name":"Byzantine","hex":"#bd33a4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Byzantine","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to Byzantium.","Belonging to the civilization of the Eastern Roman Empire between 331, when its capital was moved to Constantinople, and 1453, when that capital was conquered by the Turks and ultimately renamed Istanbul."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A native of Byzantium (modern-day Istanbul) or of the Byzantine empire","Alternative form of byzantine (coin)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Byzantine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Byzantine Empire","blocks":[{"text":"The Byzantine Empire, also known as the Eastern Roman Empire, was the continuation of the Eastern part of the Roman Empire centred on Constantinople during late antiquity and the Middle Ages. Having survived the fall of the Western Roman Empire in the 5th century AD, it endured until the fall of Constantinople to the Ottoman Empire in 1453. The term 'Byzantine Empire' was coined only after its demise; its citizens used the term 'Roman Empire' and called themselves 'Romans'."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg/330px-Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Byzantine_Empire","wikidata":"Q12544"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg/500px-Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg/1280px-Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capi